From 79143caa475d32a853710ad0e5bc2b78958c8490 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rehanraza786 <rehanraza786@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 02:41:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] Tool descriptions are for agents; the long form moves,
 it does not vanish
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The MCP tool list is re-sent to the model on every request, so description
prose is a fixed per-turn cost paid whether or not a tool is called: 6,971
bytes across 28 tools, before input schemas. TOOLS.md already carries a full
human reference, pinned to the registry by the suite, so most of that spend
was duplicating a better document into the most expensive channel available.

Descriptions now carry only what a model needs to CHOOSE: what the tool
returns, which neighbour to prefer, and any constraint that changes
behavior. 6,971 -> 3,792 bytes, identical text in both editions. What they
no longer carry is explanation, and explanation is why this commit is not
just a trim.

Three tiers, by what they cost. The tool list is paid every turn. Skills are
paid on match. TOOLS.md is paid never, or on demand. Everything cut from
tier one landed in tier two or three:

- aegis-help gains "Choosing between the lookup tools" — the three
  overlapping symbol lookups ranked by authority, why find_callers explores
  and find_references concludes, why the seam maps take no arguments, and
  what topic resolution can and cannot see. It costs nothing until a request
  routes to it, which is exactly when an agent is uncertain.
- TOOLS.md documents ${config.key} topic resolution from application*.yaml,
  which had fallen out of every reference tier when it left the description.
  That one was a real loss, found by looking rather than by assuming.

Three guards, because "nothing was lost" has to be checkable and not a
claim in a commit message: every fact that used to live in a description
must still appear in TOOLS.md or aegis-help; descriptions must be identical
across editions, since the same agent should see the same tool whichever
runtime serves it; and the budget ratchets drop to the new numbers.

Rejected: hiding primitives behind a tool profile. Every one is named by the
skills and agents — find_references in 8 files, blast_radius and
dependencies in 7 each — so a reduced surface strands the prompt layer at
runtime. Rejected too: an ariadne://tools resource, which would need
TOOLS.md shipped through two separate installers to reach the machine that
serves it; aegis-help already installs and already costs nothing until
needed.

Still unmeasured: whether an agent picks the right tool from the shorter
text. The routing eval pins skills to utterances; there is no equivalent for
tool selection, and a 46% cut deserves one.
